## 🏗 Architecture & Clean Code

**Service Container & DI Pattern Review** — Enforces constructor injection over `Facade` abuse or `app()->make()` in controllers/services. Improves testability and lifecycle management.
- **Use when:** Reviewing new services, refactoring legacy controllers, or validating unit test isolation.
- **Example:** `❌ Cache::remember(...)` in business logic → `✅ public function __construct(CacheManager $cache) { ... }`
- **Source:** https://laravel.com/docs/container

**Routing & Middleware Order Audit** — Verifies route grouping, named routes, and correct middleware sequencing. Ensures auth/permission gates execute before resource loading.
- **Use when:** Reviewing API definitions, SPA routing setups, or authentication/authorization flows.
- **Source:** https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/middleware

**Architecture & Separation of Concerns** — Identifies fat controllers, recommends Action/Service classes, and enforces domain boundaries. Prevents business logic leakage into routes or views.
- **Use when:** Reviewing complex features, onboarding new developers, or planning architectural refactors.
- **Source:** https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/controllers

**Queue & Job Design Review** — Validates retry logic, timeouts, idempotency (unique IDs/DB locks), and proper exception handling. Prevents duplicate processing and worker deadlocks.
- **Use when:** Reviewing async workflows, webhook handlers, or background task implementations.
- **Example:** `❌ public function handle() { $this->sendEmail(); }` → `✅ public function __invoke() { DB::transaction(fn () => $this->sendEmail()); }`
- **Source:** https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/queues

**Error Handling & Exception Strategy** — Checks for custom exception mapping, graceful degradation, appropriate HTTP status codes, and avoids swallowing errors in `catch` blocks.
- **Use when:** Reviewing API responses, global error handlers, or user-facing fallback flows.
- **Source:** https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/errors

